

00:00:47.751 --> 00:00:53.843
<v Bella> Hi Don. Did you get the copy of the article on recycling footwear that I emailed you?

00:00:53.843 --> 00:00:57.039
<v Don> Yeah, it's here. I've had a look at it.

00:00:57.039 --> 00:01:00.891
<v Bella> So, do you think it's a good topic for our presentation?

00:01:00.891 --> 00:01:05.903
<v Don> Well, before I started reading it, I thought, recycling footwear.

00:01:05.903 --> 00:01:11.487
<v Don> Well, although it's quite interesting, perhaps there isn't enough to say about it.

00:01:11.517 --> 00:01:17.710
<v Don> Because we put shoes in recycling bins, they go to charity shops, and that's about it.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.354
<v Bella> But there's much more to it than that.

00:01:20.354 --> 00:01:24.495
<v Don> I realise that now, and I'm keen to research the topic more.

00:01:24.495 --> 00:01:25.646
<v Bella> That's great!

00:01:25.646 --> 00:01:32.667
<v Don> One of the things I didn't realise until I read the article was just how many pairs of trainers get recycled.

00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:36.854
<v Bella> Well, a lot of young people wear them all the time now.

00:01:36.854 --> 00:01:39.819
<v Bella> They've become more popular than ordinary shoes.

00:01:39.819 --> 00:01:40.953
<v Don> I know.

00:01:40.953 --> 00:01:43.700
<v Don> I guess they are very hard-wearing.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.971
<v Don> But don't they look a bit casual for school uniform?

00:01:46.971 --> 00:01:49.008
<v Don> I don't think they're right for that.

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:53.163
<v Bella> Actually, I think some of them look quite smart on pupils.

00:01:53.163 --> 00:01:55.998
<v Bella> Better than a scruffy old pair of shoes.

00:01:55.998 --> 00:01:59.487
<v Bella> So, do you keep shoes a long time?

00:01:59.487 --> 00:02:06.464
<v Don> Yes, though I do tend to wear my old pairs for doing dirty jobs like cleaning my bike.

00:02:06.464 --> 00:02:16.712
<v Bella> I must admit, I've recycled some perfectly good shoes that haven't gone out of fashion and still fit just because they don't look great on me anymore.

00:02:16.712 --> 00:02:18.517
<v Bella> That's awful, isn't it?

00:02:18.517 --> 00:02:22.361
<v Don> I think it's common because there's so much choice.

00:02:22.361 --> 00:02:27.570
<v Don> The article did say that recent sales of footwear have increased enormously.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:29.008
<v Bella> That didn't surprise me.

00:02:29.008 --> 00:02:34.214
<v Don> No. But then it said that the amount of recycled footwear has fallen.

00:02:34.214 --> 00:02:39.737
<v Don> It's 6% now, compared to a previous level of 11%.

00:02:39.737 --> 00:02:42.070
<v Don> That doesn't seem to make sense.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:46.540
<v Bella> That's because not everything goes through the recycling process.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:53.221
<v Bella> Some footwear just isn't good enough to resell for one reason or another and gets rejected.

00:03:41.007 --> 00:03:47.635
<v Bella> So, let's find some examples in the article of footwear that was rejected for recycling.

00:03:47.635 --> 00:03:52.684
<v Don> OK. I think there are some in the interview with the recycling manager.

00:03:52.684 --> 00:03:55.747
<v Don> Yeah, here it is.

00:03:55.747 --> 00:04:01.023
<v Bella> Hmm. Let's start with the lady's high-heeled shoes.

00:04:01.023 --> 00:04:03.121
<v Bella> What did he say about those?

00:04:03.121 --> 00:04:06.867
<v Don> He said they were probably expensive.

00:04:06.867 --> 00:04:11.505
<v Don> The material was suede and they were beige in colour.

00:04:11.505 --> 00:04:21.544
<v Don> It looked like someone had only worn them once, but in a very wet field, so the heels were too stained with mud and grass to resell them.

00:04:21.544 --> 00:04:25.690
<v Bella> OK. And the leather ankle boots. 

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:27.363
<v Bella> What was wrong with them?

00:04:27.363 --> 00:04:32.160
<v Don> Apparently, the heels were worn, but that wasn't the problem.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.982
<v Don> One of the shoes was a much lighter shade than the other one.

00:04:36.041 --> 00:04:38.614
<v Don> It had obviously been left in the sun.

00:04:38.614 --> 00:04:42.015
<v Don> I suppose even second-hand shoes should look the same.

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:43.350
<v Bella> Sure.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:46.507
<v Bella> Then there were the red baby shoes. 

00:04:46.507 --> 00:04:47.946
<v Don> Oh, yes.

00:04:47.946 --> 00:04:54.941
<v Don> We're told to tie shoes together when we put them in a recycling bin, but people often don't bother.

00:04:54.941 --> 00:04:59.982
<v Bella> You'd think it would have been easy to find the other, but it wasn't.

00:04:59.982 --> 00:05:03.034
<v Bella> That was a shame because they were obviously new.

00:05:03.034 --> 00:05:06.654
<v Don> Mmm. The trainers were interesting.

00:05:06.654 --> 00:05:10.622
<v Don> He said they looked like they'd been worn by a marathon runner.

00:05:10.622 --> 00:05:13.457
<v Bella> Yeah. Weren't they split?

00:05:13.457 --> 00:05:14.809
<v Don> Not exactly.

00:05:14.809 --> 00:05:20.827
<v Don> One of the soles was so worn under the foot that you could put your finger through it.

00:05:20.827 --> 00:05:32.766
<v Don> Well, we could certainly use some of those examples in our presentation to explain why 90% of shoes that people take to recycling centers or bins get thrown into landfill.

00:05:32.766 --> 00:05:34.084
<v Bella> Mmm.

00:05:34.084 --> 00:05:42.740
<v Bella> What did you think about the project his team set up to avoid this by making new shoes out of the good parts of old shoes?

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:45.073
<v Don> It sounded like a good idea.

00:05:45.073 --> 00:05:47.428
<v Don> They get so many shoes.

00:05:47.428 --> 00:05:50.175
<v Don> They should be able to match parts.

00:05:50.175 --> 00:05:52.704
<v Don> I wasn't surprised that it failed, though.

00:05:52.704 --> 00:05:57.196
<v Don> I mean, who wants to buy second-hand shoes, really?

00:05:57.196 --> 00:05:59.775
<v Don> Think of all the germs you could catch.

00:05:59.775 --> 00:06:03.607
<v Bella> Well, people didn't refuse them for that reason, did they?

00:06:03.607 --> 00:06:07.148
<v Bella> It was because the pairs of shoes weren't identical.

00:06:07.148 --> 00:06:10.026
<v Don> They still managed to ship them overseas, though.

00:06:10.026 --> 00:06:12.972
<v Bella> That's another area we need to discuss.

00:06:12.972 --> 00:06:22.838
<v Don> You know, I used to consider this topic just from my own perspective, by thinking about my own recycling behaviour, without looking at the bigger picture.

00:06:22.838 --> 00:06:27.313
<v Don> So much happens once shoes leave the recycling area.

00:06:27.313 --> 00:06:33.916
<v Bella> It's not as simple as you first think, and we can show that by taking a very different approach to it.

00:06:33.916 --> 00:06:35.573
<v Don> Absolutely.

00:06:35.573 --> 00:06:38.842
<v Don> So, let's discuss how we're going to split up the present... 
